<v Ira Glass>So we are now at Act Two, for those of you who are keeping careful, careful score. I know there's so many of you who are. Act Two is a story about somebody who decided to follow his dream, about being a musician. And, you know, usually, when you hear stories about somebody chasing a dream like this, stories about rock stars or Olympic athletes, or writers, or painters, or basically anybody who had to get out there and follow that star, follow that dream, we don't hear how crushingly hard it is for the overwhelming majority of them. And in the middle of the story, usually, you don't wonder is this worth it?
<v Ira Glass>所以，对于那些一直在仔细计分的听众，我们现在进入第二幕。我知道你们很多人都在听。第二幕是一个关于决定追求音乐梦想的人的故事。通常，当你听到关于追逐梦想的故事，关于摇滚明星、奥运选手、作家、画家，或者任何那些出去追求星星和梦想的人，我们通常听不到这对绝大多数人来说是多么毁灭性的困难。在故事中间，你通常不会怀疑：这值得吗？

00:13:03.120 --> 00:13:15.320
<v Ira Glass>Well, the story you're about to hear does not have that shortcoming. You definitely wonder. The story you're about to hear was produced by Jay Allison with Dan Gediman. Dan is the one who narrates the story.
<v Ira Glass>那么，你们即将听到的这个故事没有这种缺陷。你肯定会怀疑的。这个故事由杰·艾利森和丹·盖迪曼制作，由丹担任讲述人。

00:13:15.320 --> 00:13:40.650
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC PLAYING - "FALL IN LOVE" BY ALEX JONES SINGING TOM JONES]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐播放 - 亚历克斯·琼斯演唱的《陷入爱河》]

00:13:40.650 --> 00:13:49.730
<v Dan Gediman>That's not Tom Jones. It's my brother, Alex Jones. And that's not his real name. His real name is Mark Gediman.
<v Dan Gediman>那不是汤姆·琼斯。是我弟弟，亚历克斯·琼斯。那也不是他的真名。他真名叫马克·盖迪曼。

00:13:49.730 --> 00:14:04.420
<v Mark Gediman>You know, my real last name is Gediman, G- E- D, as in David, I- M- A- N. You know, I'm proud of the name. But, as far as show biz is concerned, it can't be spelled.
<v Mark Gediman>你知道，我的真姓是盖迪曼，G-E-D-I-M-A-N。我对这个姓很自豪。但就演艺圈而言，它太难拼了。

00:14:04.420 --> 00:14:08.260
<v Dan Gediman>My brother's middle name is Alex. He bumped it up to the front.
<v Dan Gediman>我弟弟的中间名是亚历克斯。他把它挪到了前面。

00:14:08.260 --> 00:14:25.600
<v Mark Gediman>But I like Alex. Alexes work. There aren't too many Alexes that are big these days, so I'm sticking with that name. It's very catchy because people always remember Alex.
<v Mark Gediman>但我喜欢亚历克斯。亚历克斯这个名字好使。现在没多少大牌叫亚历克斯，所以我坚持用这个名字。它很抓耳，因为人们总是能记住亚历克斯。

00:14:25.600 --> 00:14:37.680
<v Dan Gediman>For a while, when he was in this rock band in the late '70s, he called himself Alex Space. But that's another story. For the past five years it's been Jones, Alex Jones.
<v Dan Gediman>有一段时间，当他在70年代末参加那个摇滚乐队时，他管自己叫“亚历克斯空间”。但那是另一个故事了。过去五年里，他一直叫琼斯，亚历克斯·琼斯。

00:14:37.680 --> 00:14:42.970
<v Mark Gediman>I had a jones for singing. I had an addiction.
<v Mark Gediman>我有一种唱歌的瘾。我上瘾了。

00:14:42.970 --> 00:14:52.880
<v Dan Gediman>That's pretty much the truth. My brother has had a jones for singing for just about as long as he's been alive. All he ever wanted to do is make music, to sing for people.
<v Dan Gediman>这基本是实话。我弟弟从他记事起就对唱歌上瘾。他想要的只是创作音乐，为人们唱歌。

00:14:52.880 --> 00:15:03.610
<v Mark Gediman>The Kiwanis group, the Knights of Columbus, the Lions Club, the Elks, the Sons of Italy, you name it, if there's one of those, I've been there.
<v Mark Gediman>基瓦尼会、哥伦布骑士会、狮子会、麋鹿会、意大利之子会，不管是什么组织，只要有的地方，我都去过。

00:15:03.610 --> 00:15:17.300
<v Dan Gediman>My brother sang constantly in our house when we were growing up. He always wanted to be a rock star or the next great soul singer. Once he was out on his own, he sang everywhere he could. And he still does, anywhere he can find an audience to appreciate him.
<v Dan Gediman>我弟弟小时候在家里一直在唱歌。他总是想成为摇滚明星或是下一位伟大的灵魂乐歌手。他独立生活后，能去的地方都唱。现在也一样，只要能找到欣赏他的听众，他就会唱。

00:15:17.300 --> 00:15:34.150
<v Mark Gediman>--the Jerry Lewis Telethon. I've done schools. I've done colleges. I've done arts exhibition and whatever-- they also have cows and things like that, you know, one of those-- and, basically, anybody that'll have me, you know? Yeah, Tom gets around.
<v Mark Gediman>——杰瑞·路易斯马拉松慈善节目。我去过学校、学院，参加过艺术展之类的——有些地方还有奶牛之类的，你知道，那种——基本上，任何人愿意让我去，我就去，你知道吗？是的，汤姆经常到处跑。

00:15:34.150 --> 00:15:35.630
<v Mark Gediman>[LAUGHTER]
<v Mark Gediman>[笑声]

00:15:35.630 --> 00:15:47.990
<v Dan Gediman>My brother's been in countless rock, blues, and R&B bands over the years. But, these days, he's mostly hired as a Tom Jones impersonator. He does that by night, though. During the day, he holds down a straight job.
<v Dan Gediman>这些年来，我弟弟参加过无数摇滚、蓝调和R&B乐队。但这些天，他主要是被聘为汤姆·琼斯的模仿者。不过，他是在晚上做这些。白天，他有一份正经工作。

00:15:47.990 --> 00:16:10.180
<v Mark Gediman>You know, it's tough to tell people, your co-workers, I'm going to be Tom Jones, particularly when I've been trying for the last few years to develop an image of this-- I've been working as a computer analyst-- and developing this image as this kind of conservative nerd-type. And I've totally, totally blown it.
<v Mark Gediman>你知道，要告诉同事“我要去做汤姆·琼斯”很难，尤其是当我过去几年一直在树立一种形象——我一直做计算机分析师——树立这种保守书呆子的形象。我把这一切全搞砸了。

00:16:10.180 --> 00:16:27.960
<v Mark Gediman>[SINGING - "FALL IN LOVE" AS TOM JONES]
<v Mark Gediman>[演唱 - 作为汤姆·琼斯的《陷入爱河》]

00:16:27.960 --> 00:16:48.870
<v Dan Gediman>My brother performs with a revue called The Hall of Fame Superstars. There's a Patsy Cline, an Elton John, Buddy Holly, Roy Orbison. And then my brother closes the show as Tom Jones.
<v Dan Gediman>我弟弟在一个叫“名人堂超级巨星”的剧团演出。里面有帕特西·克莱恩、埃尔顿·约翰、巴迪·霍利、罗伊·奥比森。最后由我弟弟作为汤姆·琼斯压轴出场。

00:16:48.870 --> 00:17:06.880
<v Mark Gediman>Singing as Tom Jones is very similar to the way that I naturally sing. We have a lot in common. But I've gotten to the point where I'm hitting notes and doing things that are beyond the scope of what he did.
<v Mark Gediman>唱汤姆·琼斯的歌和我自然的唱歌方式很像。我们有很多共同点。但我已经达到了那种能唱出更高音符、做到他原本范围之外事情的程度。

00:17:06.880 --> 00:17:19.670
<v Dan Gediman>My brother is nothing, if not confident, at least on the surface. He thinks positive and has an uncanny, almost, overconfidence. It's an amazing quality, really, even unsettling.
<v Dan Gediman>我弟弟如果说有什么特点，那就是自信，至少在表面上。他心态积极，有一种离奇的、近乎过度的自信。这真的很惊人，甚至让人感到不安。

00:17:19.670 --> 00:17:31.370
<v Dan Gediman>My brother's 43, not married, doesn't have kids. He's had a lot of girlfriends, but he doesn't have a steady one now. These days, he's living at home with my parents in Massachusetts.
<v Dan Gediman>我弟弟今年43岁，没结婚，没孩子。他有过很多女朋友，但现在没有稳定的。这些天，他住在马萨诸塞州我父母的家里。

00:17:31.370 --> 00:17:33.710
<v Dan Gediman>Why don't we go to the music room?
<v Dan Gediman>我们要不去音乐室？

00:17:33.710 --> 00:17:34.200
<v Mark Gediman>All right.
<v Mark Gediman>好的。

00:17:34.200 --> 00:17:37.100
<v Dan Gediman>--for a little bit to get out of the way.
<v Dan Gediman>——避开一会儿。

00:17:37.100 --> 00:17:40.140
<v Ira Glass>Growing up, we had a room in the house we called the music room.
<v Ira Glass>成长过程中，我们家里有一个房间叫音乐室。

00:17:40.140 --> 00:17:42.180
<v Dan Gediman>Now it looks a whole lot different.
<v Dan Gediman>现在看起来大不相同了。

00:17:42.180 --> 00:17:44.800
<v Ira Glass>That's where my brother and I talked the last time I was home visiting.
<v Ira Glass>这就是我上次回家探亲时，我和我弟弟聊天的地方。

00:17:44.800 --> 00:17:46.260
<v Mark Gediman>There was a Scott amplifier.
<v Mark Gediman>那里有一个斯科特放大器。

00:17:46.260 --> 00:17:48.170
<v Dan Gediman>Right. Right in the corner here.
<v Dan Gediman>对。就在角落里。

00:17:48.170 --> 00:17:57.220
<v Mark Gediman>Yeah, and in the corner here was a very large, professional turntable, which had come right from a radio station. And I forget what the brand of that one was.
<v Mark Gediman>是的，角落里还有一个非常专业的大唱片机，直接从广播电台弄来的。我忘了是什么牌子了。

00:17:57.220 --> 00:17:57.460
<v Dan Gediman>Rek-O-Kut.
<v Dan Gediman>Rek-O-Kut。

00:17:57.460 --> 00:18:02.310
<v Mark Gediman>Rek-O-Kut, yeah. But it was a top-of-the-line.
<v Mark Gediman>Rek-O-Kut，是的。那可是顶级的。

00:18:02.310 --> 00:18:24.330
<v Mark Gediman>But, yeah, this was a great room, great atmosphere. And you walked in this room and there were little musical notes bouncing all around it. And it was a place where we could just go in and play around and make believe you're the star.
<v Mark Gediman>但没错，这是个很棒的房间，氛围很好。你走进这个房间，到处都是跳动的音符。这是一个我们可以进去假装自己是明星的地方。

00:18:24.330 --> 00:18:41.380
<v Mark Gediman>[SINGING] Come on, baby. Oh, let's loop-a-loo. She's a ha-hum, baby. Nothin' she can do. She's my little baby. Little Latin Lupe Loo.
<v Mark Gediman>[演唱] 来吧，宝贝。噢，让我们摇摆吧。她是位……没什么能难倒她。她是我的小宝贝。小小的拉丁女孩。

00:18:41.380 --> 00:18:55.460
<v Dan Gediman>This is a tape of my brother pretending to be a star, recorded on my family's old Grundig tape recorder back around 1966. My parents put a lot of insulation in the walls of the music room, so my brother could really scream.
<v Dan Gediman>这是我弟弟假装成明星的一盘磁带，大约1966年在我家那台旧Grundig录音机上录的。我父母在音乐室的墙壁里加了很多隔音材料，这样我弟弟就可以尽情地尖叫。

00:18:55.460 --> 00:19:30.270
<v Mark Gediman>The entertainers who really are memorable and who've stand the test of time, a great deal of them have tremendous screams. So I've always thought that screaming was essential to someone who-- not necessarily in the popular music idiom. But, in anything that had any kind of edge or soul to it, there's got to be a scream there, somewhere. And the whole reason is the same reason. It's a expression of the most inner, primal feelings.
<v Mark Gediman>那些真正令人难忘、经得起时间考验的艺人，很多人都有惊人的尖叫功底。所以我一直认为尖叫对一个人来说是必不可少的——并不一定是在流行音乐习语中。但在任何有某种底色或灵魂的东西中，某处必须得有一声尖叫。而其原因都是一样的。那是内心深处原始情感的一种表达。

00:19:30.270 --> 00:19:34.600
<v Mark Gediman>Wow! Hey! Oh!
<v Mark Gediman>哇！嘿！噢！

00:19:34.600 --> 00:19:47.980
<v Dan Gediman>This is the room where my brother began studying the great rhythm and blues singers, people like Al Green, Little Richard, Otis Redding, James Brown, and Tom Jones, who my brother thinks brings them all together.
<v Dan Gediman>这就是我弟弟开始研究那些伟大的节奏蓝调歌手的房间，像艾尔·格林、小理查德、奥蒂斯·雷丁、詹姆斯·布朗，还有汤姆·琼斯，我弟弟认为他把这些人都融合在了一起。

00:19:47.980 --> 00:19:51.400
<v Mark Gediman>Just at the beginning of What's New Pussycat, he goes, "What's!"
<v Mark Gediman>就在《What's New Pussycat》开头，他喊道：“What's!”

00:19:51.400 --> 00:19:52.190
<v Dan Gediman>Right.
<v Dan Gediman>对。

00:19:52.190 --> 00:19:57.310
<v Mark Gediman>See? Now, you're getting, now, right there with the what;s, you've got James Brown, "Wow!" You know?
<v Mark Gediman>你看？就在那个What's里，你有詹姆斯·布朗的“Wow！”你知道吧？

00:19:57.310 --> 00:19:57.700
<v Dan Gediman>Right.
<v Dan Gediman>对。

00:19:57.700 --> 00:20:21.100
<v Mark Gediman>And then you got Wilson Pickett, "Ah-Ow!" And then you got Ray Charles, "Mm, Oh!" You know? And then you've got like, "Yeah!" Then you get, "Uh!" and, "Ah!" and, "Hey! Hoy! Ah! Ah-oh! Ew-oh! Oh! Mm! Mm! What's!" See? So that's there, you know?
<v Mark Gediman>然后你有威尔逊·皮克特的“Ah-Ow！”然后你有雷·查尔斯的“Mm, Oh！”你知道吧？然后你有那种“Yeah！”然后是“Uh！”和“Ah！”还有“Hey! Hoy! Ah! Ah-oh! Ew-oh! Oh! Mm! Mm! What's!”你看？这就是那种感觉，你知道吧？

00:20:21.100 --> 00:20:28.940
<v Dan Gediman>I remember the first time I ever saw my brother perform. My parents took me to see him at a high school variety show when I was still in elementary school.
<v Dan Gediman>我还记得我第一次见到我弟弟表演。在我上小学的时候，父母带我去高中综艺晚会看他表演。

00:20:28.940 --> 00:20:56.340
<v Mark Gediman>Now, nobody knew that I could sing. But there was a band, the Blood, Sweat & Tears, and the first version of them with Al Cooper. And there was a song on there called, "I Love You More Than You'll Ever Know", which is a real bluesy ballad. And I used to sing that over, and over, and over again in this music room. And so I ended up singing it.
<v Mark Gediman>当时没人知道我能唱歌。但是有一个叫“血、汗与泪”的乐队，是艾尔·库珀参与的第一版。里面有一首歌叫《I Love You More Than You'll Ever Know》，那是一首非常蓝调的慢歌。我过去在那间音乐室一遍又一遍地唱这首歌。所以最后我就唱了这首。

00:20:56.340 --> 00:21:32.070
<v Mark Gediman>It was my first real performance, as me, in front of a stage, being the performer, like the Elvis or something. And I can remember having this silky shirt on. And I probably had my ID bracelet hanging. And I opened up my collar a little. And it was quite an experience, as I recall. And I think I did fairly well. I still do. I really sold it out, you know? I really started screaming there at the end.
<v Mark Gediman>那是我第一次真正地在舞台前作为表演者出现，像猫王什么的。我记得我穿着一件丝绸衬衫。我的身份证手链可能也挂在外面。我把领口解开了点。回想起来，那真是一次难得的经历。我觉得我演得还不错。我真的卖力了，你知道吗？在结尾的时候我真的开始尖叫了。

00:21:32.070 --> 00:21:41.330
<v Dan Gediman>I remember, by the end of the number, your shirt was virtually half off or something. That was my recollection. I was very impressed. I'm sure it was the first time I ever saw you perform.
<v Dan Gediman>我记得在曲子结束时，你的衬衫几乎都掉了一半。那是我的记忆。我印象很深。我敢肯定那是第一次看你演出。

00:21:41.330 --> 00:21:43.120
<v Mark Gediman>I don't even remember that you were there.
<v Mark Gediman>我甚至都不记得你在场。

00:21:43.120 --> 00:21:44.370
<v Dan Gediman>Oh, I was absolutely there.
<v Dan Gediman>噢，我绝对在场。

00:21:44.370 --> 00:21:44.880
<v Mark Gediman>Yeah?
<v Mark Gediman>是吗？

00:21:44.880 --> 00:21:49.360
<v Dan Gediman>Yeah, absolutely there. And I remember that very well. And mostly, of course, I remember you.
<v Dan Gediman>是的，绝对在。我记忆犹新。当然，我主要记得的是你。

00:21:49.360 --> 00:22:06.300
<v Dan Gediman>I have a vivid memory of being on the playground behind my elementary school, trying to explain to the other kids that, the night before, my brother was this big star. And I imitated you, singing the same song, which, of course, I had heard endlessly with you rehearsing it.
<v Dan Gediman>我至今记得在小学校园的操场上，试图向其他孩子解释，昨晚我弟弟是个大明星。我模仿你唱歌，那首歌，我因为听你反复排练，当然已经听得烂熟于心。

00:22:06.300 --> 00:22:14.450
<v Dan Gediman>And I still know the song. And I went out and bought the record, by the way, about five years ago. And I have it. And the only reason I know that album is because of you.
<v Dan Gediman>我现在还会唱那首歌。顺便说一下，五年前我买了那张唱片。我有这张碟。我了解那张专辑完全是因为你。

00:22:14.450 --> 00:22:29.720
<v Dan Gediman>But, I remember, I was imitating you complete with undoing my shirt. I felt like a celebrity, even though the other kids didn't know it, because, god, my brother, he just did this thing. And so I was pretty impressed.
<v Dan Gediman>但我记得我当时模仿你，甚至连解开衬衫的动作都做了。我感觉自己像个名人，尽管其他孩子不知道，因为，天呐，我弟弟刚才做到了那个，所以我深受震撼。

00:22:29.720 --> 00:22:52.690
<v Ira Glass>If it's not clear by now, I idolized my brother. I used to sneak into his room when he wasn't home and look through his things, put on his clothes, smell his cologne. As a matter of fact, I still have a YMCA T-shirt that he used to wear when he was a counselor at Camp Beaver. It's full of holes, almost a rag, but I'd never let it go.
<v Ira Glass>如果现在还不清楚，我曾崇拜我弟弟。他不在家时，我会偷偷溜进他的房间，翻看他的东西，穿他的衣服，闻他的香水。事实上，我至今还留着一件YMCA的T恤，他当河狸夏令营辅导员时穿的。上面满是洞，几乎成了抹布，但我绝不丢弃。

00:22:52.690 --> 00:23:03.770
<v Ira Glass>And I've always followed his career in music because I was sure, especially after high school, that he was going to make the big-time. And he almost did. His close calls with fame were many.
<v Ira Glass>我一直关注他的音乐生涯，因为我确信，尤其在高中毕业后，他会出人头地的。他也确实差点成功了。他与名声擦肩而过的次数很多。

00:23:03.770 --> 00:23:18.820
<v Mark Gediman>They proceeded to tell us that they were going to put something like a half a million dollars behind The Super Group. And we were going to be the next Beatles or Monkeys. And I think, at the time, I was 21, I believe. And I'm just sitting there going, "Wow. I made the big-time."
<v Mark Gediman>他们接着告诉我们，他们要在“超级乐队”身上投入大约50万美元。我们将成为下一个披头士或顽童乐队。当时我好像21岁。我坐在那儿想：“哇。我终于成功了。”

00:23:18.820 --> 00:23:25.800
<v Mark Gediman>And he actually said to me that, "I think you're the next Bruce Springsteen." He, at the time, was an A&R representative for Atlantic Records.
<v Mark Gediman>他实际上对我说：“我觉得你是下一个布鲁斯·斯普林斯汀。”他当时是大西洋唱片的A&R代表。

00:23:25.800 --> 00:23:27.610
<v Mark Gediman>Joining this band called, Easy Action.
<v Mark Gediman>加入那个叫“Easy Action”的乐队。

00:23:27.610 --> 00:23:35.240
<v Mark Gediman>They said, "Listen, we want 50% of you." And I said, "Forget it." I started a band right after that called Alex Space and the Orbits.
<v Mark Gediman>他们说：“听着，我们要你50%的份额。”我说：“想都别想。”在那之后，我马上组建了一个乐队，叫“亚历克斯空间与轨道”。

00:23:35.240 --> 00:23:37.120
<v Mark Gediman>"We're going to take you to the top."
<v Mark Gediman>“我们要把你送上巅峰。”

