00:36:28.800 --> 00:37:13.580
<v Ira Glass>Says this ad, "According to recent studies, by the year 2001 there will be more people in bands than the global economy can ever hope to support. The US Census Bureau puts the current number of audience members to band members at a ratio of 2:1. The present trends threatened to reverse this relationship, disrupting the delicate balance between performers and normal people. Soon, not only will virtually everyone be in a band, but everyone in a band will be involved in several side projects, creating a glut of virtually indistinguishable groups of guitar-wielding miscreants who, when not performing their so-called music, will be endlessly talking about it to anyone unable to flee."
<v Ira Glass>这则广告写道：“根据最新研究，到2001年，从事乐队工作的人数将超过全球经济所能支撑的极限。美国人口普查局目前的观众与乐队成员比例为2:1。当前趋势威胁要逆转这一比例，破坏表演者与普通人之间的脆弱平衡。很快，不仅几乎每个人都会身处某支乐队，而且每个人都会参与好几个副业项目，制造出一大堆几乎无法区分的、挥舞着吉他的败类组合。当他们不在表演所谓的音乐时，他们会无休止地向任何无法逃脱的人谈论音乐。”

00:37:13.580 --> 00:37:33.590
<v Ira Glass>And then the ad explains what's going to happen at the conference. The seminars dealing with the imminent dangers of band overpopulation, Legal Strategies for Achieving Zero Band Population Growth, Private Sector Initiatives for Ending Band Growth, Strategies for Ending Multiple Band Membership.
<v Ira Glass>广告随后解释了会议的内容。有关乐队人口过剩迫在眉睫的研讨会、实现零乐队人口增长的法律策略、结束乐队增长的私营部门举措、结束多个乐队成员身份的策略。

00:37:33.590 --> 00:38:07.720
<v Ira Glass>Here's some of the things that they suggest can be done to combat this global crisis, let's see, three-strikes-and-you're-out rules; creating stiff criminal penalties for artists who release more than three full-length albums per career; waiting periods for instrument purchasers to allow for mandatory background checks for prior infractions, and a cooling-off period to discourage thoughtless and impulsive band formation; stiff fines for bands that imitate other bands; civil infractions for air bands, and an all-out ban on cover bands.
<v Ira Glass>以下是他们建议采取的应对全球危机的措施，让我们看看：三振出局规则；为发行超过三张全长专辑的艺术家制定严厉的刑事处罚；为购买乐器者设定等待期，以便进行强制性的违规行为背景调查，并设定冷静期以遏制轻率和冲动的组队行为；对模仿其他乐队的乐队课以重罚；对“空气乐队”处以民事罚款，并全面禁止翻唱乐队。

00:38:07.720 --> 00:38:39.190
<v Ira Glass>They also suggest-- let's see-- boycotting labels that refuse to stop signing additional bands as well as cracking down on pointless, local, so-called, indie labels; finally, supporting random band testing by employers, especially in high-risk businesses like coffee shops, copy shops, cafes, bars, and record stores.
<v Ira Glass>他们还建议——看看——抵制拒绝停止签约额外乐队的厂牌，并打击毫无意义的、所谓的当地独立厂牌；最后，支持雇主对乐队成员进行随机测试，特别是在咖啡馆、打印店、餐厅、酒吧和唱片店等高风险企业。

00:38:39.190 --> 00:39:15.520
<v Ira Glass>Well, given the crisis of global band population explosion, perhaps the only honorable course for all of us is to forget about performing and just put that energy into being members of the audience. And what we present next on our program, as Act Three, is a case example of how to do exactly that.
<v Ira Glass>好吧，鉴于全球乐队人口爆炸的危机，或许我们所有人唯一光荣的路就是忘记表演，把精力投入到成为观众成员中。而我们接下来在节目中作为第三幕呈现的，正是如何做到这一点的一个案例。

00:39:15.520 --> 00:39:40.530
<v Ira Glass>This is the Fastbacks, a pop, punk band from Seattle that's been around for years, actually. And the story you're about to hear is not about being in a semi-obscure but well-respected band, it's about being a fan of a semi-obscure but well-respected band. Sarah Vowell is a writer and a music columnist for the San Francisco Weekly , and she tells the story.
<v Ira Glass>这是西雅图的一支流行朋克乐队，名叫“Fastbacks”，他们已经存在很多年了。你们即将听到的故事不是关于身处一支半冷门但备受尊敬的乐队，而是关于成为一支半冷门但备受尊敬的乐队的粉丝。萨拉·沃威尔是一位作家，也是《旧金山周刊》的音乐专栏作家，她讲述了这个故事。

00:39:40.530 --> 00:39:56.080
<v Sarah Vowell>Sometimes, opening your mail can be a little like listening to the radio. You're not paying attention, just absent-mindedly flipping through the letters and bills, and then, wham, some little surprise comes out of nowhere and changes your day.
<v Sarah Vowell>有时候，拆开信件有点像听广播。你没注意，只是心不在焉地翻看着信件和账单，然后，砰，一些小惊喜突然出现，改变了你的一天。

00:39:56.080 --> 00:40:14.680
<v Sarah Vowell>I got this package. Inside was a letter, thanking me for mentioning the Fastbacks in a book review I had written, not from one of the Fastbacks themselves, mind you, just some guy in Chicago. His name was Scott Lee, and he'd enclosed this xerox booklet about the band.
<v Sarah Vowell>我收到了这个包裹。里面有一封信，感谢我在书评中提到了Fastbacks，不是来自乐队成员本人，只是芝加哥的一个家伙。他叫斯科特·李，还附上了一本关于乐队的影印小册子。

00:40:14.680 --> 00:40:59.730
<v Sarah Vowell>Flipping through it, I stopped cold at something called, "The Fastback's Drummers Pie Chart". Since they formed in 1979, the Fastbacks have gone through about a dozen drummers. And this pie chart identifies the drummers by name, listing the percentage of songs which each one has contributed to. But, at the bottom of the page, small print warns, "Note, Rusty and Nate's shares may not be totally accurate. Their percentages may be approximately 0.68% off." And I don't think a decimal point had ever struck me as endearing before. The drastic exactitude of 0.68% sort of captured my heart, and I had to meet this person.
<v Sarah Vowell>翻看时，我在一个叫“Fastbacks鼓手饼图”的地方停住了。自1979年组建以来，Fastbacks已经换了十几位鼓手。这个饼图按名字列出了鼓手，列出了每位鼓手贡献歌曲的百分比。但在页面底部，小字警示道：“注：拉斯蒂和内特的份额可能不完全准确。他们的百分比可能误差约0.68%。”我不认为小数点点位曾经让我觉得如此可爱。那种0.68%的严谨简直俘获了我的心，我必须见见这个人。

00:40:59.730 --> 00:41:14.830
<v Scott Lee>Basically, it has your standard discography. And then there's just some facts here that I dug out, 139 total songs, eight hours, 25 minutes, 43 seconds worth of songs.
<v Scott Lee>基本上，它有标准的唱片目录。还有一些我挖出来的统计数据，总共139首歌，八小时、25分、43秒的音乐总量。

00:41:14.830 --> 00:41:26.530
<v Sarah Vowell>So he showed me other stats, like total recorded versions of songs, total number of seconds of songs, which is 30,343, in case you were wondering.
<v Sarah Vowell>所以他向我展示了其他数据，比如总共录制的版本数，歌曲总秒数，如果你好奇的话，是30,343秒。

00:41:26.530 --> 00:41:34.670
<v Scott Lee>--shortest song, shortest instrumental song, shortest song with vocals, the longest song, and the longest song without a drum solo. That's only because--
<v Scott Lee>——最短的歌，最短的器乐歌曲，最短的带人声歌曲，最长的歌，以及最长的不带鼓独奏的歌。这只是因为——

00:41:34.670 --> 00:41:56.450
<v Sarah Vowell>I was stunned. I mean, I thought I liked the band. And, normally, as a music lover and as a woman, I rail against the mostly-male, record-collector geek habit of reducing rock and roll to baseball card collecting. They flatten out this complex, thrilling thing down to manageable lists of names and titles and dates.
<v Sarah Vowell>我惊呆了。我是说，我以为我喜欢这支乐队。通常情况下，作为一个音乐爱好者和女性，我反感那种以男性为主的唱片收集怪癖，他们把摇滚乐简化为收集棒球卡。他们把这种复杂、令人兴奋的东西压平为可管理的姓名、标题和日期列表。

00:41:56.450 --> 00:42:15.860
<v Sarah Vowell>But Scott Lee's Fastbacks tribute has the devotional fervour of a medieval illuminated manuscript. The list of facts and figures is so relentless and exact and painstaking that it takes on this liturgical glow, as if the Fastbacks were a religion and Scott Lee its prophet.
<v Sarah Vowell>但斯科特·李对Fastbacks的致敬却有一种中世纪泥金手抄本般的虔诚。这些事实和数据列得如此无情、严谨且呕心沥血，以至于它带上了一种礼仪般的光辉，仿佛Fastbacks是一种宗教，而斯科特·李就是先知。

00:42:15.860 --> 00:42:46.810
<v Scott Lee>But some of the new additions to this-- as I ramble here-- listed out the songs A through Z and did the percentages of songs starting with each letter. And the letter that won was I. There were 19 Fastback songs that started with I, representing about 14% of the songs. With every album, they seem to have a song that starts with, "In", like, "In America", "In The Summer", "In The Winter", "In The Observatory".
<v Scott Lee>但其中一些新的补充——当我在这儿闲谈时——列出了从A到Z的歌曲，并统计了每个字母开头的歌曲百分比。胜出的字母是I。Fastbacks有19首歌以I开头，占歌曲总数的14%左右。每张专辑，他们似乎都有以“In”开头的歌，比如“In America”、“In The Summer”、“In The Winter”、“In The Observatory”。

00:42:46.810 --> 00:43:12.820
<v Sarah Vowell>I soon found out that the band has befriended Scott Lee and that they've sort of informally appointed him their de-facto archivist. Singer Kim Warnick corresponds with Scott via email, sometimes, like four times a day. Guitarist and songwriter, Kurt Bloch, graciously calls him "A good person to have on our team." Though, when he first saw Scott Lee's packet, he had some understandable worries.
<v Sarah Vowell>我很快发现乐队已经和斯科特·李交了朋友，他们已经非正式地任命他为事实上的档案管理员。歌手金·沃尼克有时通过电子邮件与斯科特沟通，甚至每天四次。吉他手兼词曲作者库尔特·布洛赫优雅地称他为“我们团队里很好的人”。不过，当他第一次看到斯科特·李的资料包时，他有一些可以理解的担忧。

00:43:12.820 --> 00:43:54.100
<v Kurt Bloch>Ah! I mean, it was like, boy. The first thing that would come to my mind is, is this guy really scary? Is he the kind of person I would run away from, if he was ever in the same room? Is this the kind of person that I would stop answering my telephone if he called? And, no, it's not. Not to make you think that we don't think Scott Lee is actually fairly crazy of a person, but he's not the annoying kind of crazy person that you read about. And I think he appreciates his own over-the-top-ness.
<v Kurt Bloch>啊！我的意思是，当时的想法是，天呐。我脑子里冒出的第一件事是，这家伙真的可怕吗？如果他出现在同一个房间里，他会是我那种想逃跑的人吗？如果他打电话，我会拒绝接听吗？不，不是。并不是说我不觉得斯科特·李本人相当疯狂，但他不是那种你在新闻里读到的那种令人讨厌的疯狂。而且我觉得他自己也意识到了这种过火。

00:43:54.100 --> 00:44:04.570
<v Sarah Vowell>If anything, the Fastbacks are a little protective of Scott Lee. When I was talking to Kurt, I jokingly called Scott his stalker. But he set me straight.
<v Sarah Vowell>如果有的话，Fastbacks对斯科特·李还有点保护欲。当我和库尔特谈话时，我开玩笑地称斯科特为他的跟踪狂。但他纠正了我。

00:44:04.570 --> 00:44:06.660
<v Kurt Bloch>No, he's not a stalker. He's a super-fan.
<v Kurt Bloch>不，他不是跟踪狂。他是超级粉丝。

00:44:06.660 --> 00:44:07.760
<v Sarah Vowell>Oh, a super-fan?
<v Sarah Vowell>噢，超级粉丝？

00:44:07.760 --> 00:44:15.720
<v Kurt Bloch>Yeah. Stalker, that has negative connotations. Yeah, that's something we don't need. Super-fan is fine.
<v Kurt Bloch>是的。跟踪狂有消极含义。是的，那是不必要的。超级粉丝很好。

00:44:15.720 --> 00:44:36.690
<v Sarah Vowell>Like the super-fan, the members of the Fastbacks all have day jobs. So, on any given day, it's possible that Scott is devoting more energy to being a fan than the Fastbacks devote to being in the band. When I interviewed Kim, the gaping differences between being a band member and being a super-fan were never clearer.
<v Sarah Vowell>像这位超级粉丝一样，Fastbacks的成员都有白天的工作。所以，在任何一天，斯科特投入作为粉丝的精力可能比Fastbacks投入乐队的时间还要多。当我采访金时，身为乐队成员和超级粉丝之间的巨大差异从未如此清晰。

00:44:36.690 --> 00:44:39.940
<v Sarah Vowell>OK, so I have a pop-quiz for you.
<v Sarah Vowell>OK，我要考你一个快问快答。

00:44:39.940 --> 00:44:40.340
<v Kim Warnick>OK.
<v Kim Warnick>OK。

00:44:40.340 --> 00:44:49.110
<v Sarah Vowell>How many total songs, before the new album, New Mansions in Sound , have the Fastbacks recorded?
<v Sarah Vowell>在新专辑《New Mansions in Sound》之前，Fastbacks总共录制了多少首歌？

00:44:49.110 --> 00:44:51.110
<v Kim Warnick>What is it, now?
<v Kim Warnick>现在是多少来着？

00:44:51.110 --> 00:44:54.220
<v Sarah Vowell>How many total songs have the Fastbacks recorded?
<v Sarah Vowell>Fastbacks总共录制了多少首歌？

00:44:54.220 --> 00:44:58.190
<v Kim Warnick>Well, you know, unfortunately, I don't have something in front of me that could answer that question.
<v Kim Warnick>好吧，不幸的是，我手头没有能回答那个问题的资料。

00:44:58.190 --> 00:45:06.960
<v Sarah Vowell>Really? OK. I have to say it is 127. And I was wondering also if you knew the median song length?
<v Sarah Vowell>真的？好吧。我得说那是127首。我还想知道你是否知道中位数时长？

00:45:06.960 --> 00:45:08.510
<v Kim Warnick>The what?
<v Kim Warnick>什么？

00:45:08.510 --> 00:45:14.750
<v Sarah Vowell>Well, let's start with an easier one. What do you think is the average length of a Fastbacks song?
<v Sarah Vowell>好吧，从简单的开始吧。你觉得Fastbacks歌曲的平均长度是多少？

00:45:14.750 --> 00:45:28.420
<v Ira Glass>She doesn't know. But, according to Scott, it's two minutes, 51 seconds.
<v Ira Glass>她不知道。但根据斯科特的数据，是两分51秒。

00:45:28.420 --> 00:45:55.560
<v Ira Glass>When I visit Scott Lee's house on Chicago's north side, his roommate lets me in. Scott's playing his guitar along with the new Fastbacks album. His room is decorated with Fastbacks posters. And Scott himself wears a Fastbacks T-shirt. He shows me this incredibly complex chart he's working on, a sort of family tree to the Seattle music scene, a town, by the way, which he has only visited once.
<v Ira Glass>当我造访斯科特·李位于芝加哥北区的房子时，他的室友让我进去了。斯科特正跟着Fastbacks的新专辑弹吉他。他的房间装饰着Fastbacks的海报。斯科特本人穿着Fastbacks的T恤。他向我展示了他正在研究的极其复杂的图表，一种西雅图音乐场景的家谱，顺便提一下，那个城市他只去过一次。

00:45:55.560 --> 00:46:22.890
<v Ira Glass>The producers of This American Life would like me to say here that, when Scott talks about the Fastbacks, he becomes beautiful because that would go along with the first piece in the show today. But I would never say something so corny.
<v Ira Glass>《美国生活》的制作人希望我在这里说，当斯科特谈论Fastbacks时，他的脸变得很美，因为这会呼应今天节目中的第一部分。但我绝不会说出如此老套的话。

00:46:22.890 --> 00:46:32.010
<v Sarah Vowell>When do you think you made the switch between just being a listener and being, as Kurt Bloch called you today, a super-fan?
<v Sarah Vowell>你觉得你是在什么时候从一个普通的听众变成了库尔特·布洛赫今天所说的“超级粉丝”？

00:46:32.010 --> 00:47:10.700
<v Scott Lee>I think the transition really happened when I was in law school, and I had made this tape of my favorite Fastbacks songs. And I was walking across a very icy, cold midway in Buffalo. And, despite how cold it was, when I listened to this music, it really just pumped all this life into me. It was really a life-affirming experience, as corny as that might sound. But, I don't know, I was just really happy at that point. And it was just a light on me that shown. And I hadn't seen it before. And now everything seemed better.
<v Scott Lee>我觉得那个转变真的发生在我读法学院的时候，当时我制作了一盘我最喜欢的Fastbacks歌曲的磁带。我正走过布法罗一个冰冷刺骨的中途站。尽管天气很冷，但当我听这音乐时，它真的给我的生命注入了活力。这真的是一次肯定生命的经历，听起来可能很老套。但我不知道，在那一刻我真的非常快乐。在那之前我从未见过那样的光亮，而现在，一切似乎都变得更好了。

00:47:10.700 --> 00:47:20.200
<v Sarah Vowell>When you show this info packet to people, what is usually their reaction?
<v Sarah Vowell>当你把这个资料包展示给人们时，他们的反应通常是什么？

00:47:20.200 --> 00:47:26.880
<v Scott Lee>I think the first question I always hear is, "Do you have a lot of spare time?"
<v Scott Lee>我想我听到的第一个问题总是：“你有很多闲暇时间吗？”

00:47:26.880 --> 00:47:50.860
<v Sarah Vowell>In fact, Scott's spare time is limited. He holds down a full-time job at a Chicago advertising agency, and he writes his own songs. And, truthfully, he flirts with the idea of not staying a fan. He's moving to Seattle, partly to be closer to the Fastbacks. He's actually in their circle of friends now. And he's tried a couple of times to start his own band.
<v Sarah Vowell>事实上，斯科特的闲暇时间很有限。他在芝加哥的一家广告代理公司有一份全职工作，而且他自己也在写歌。说实话，他曾动过不再做粉丝的念头。他正要搬到西雅图，部分原因是为了离Fastbacks更近。他现在确实在他们的朋友圈里了。而且他试过几次想组建自己的乐队。

00:47:50.860 --> 00:48:05.510
<v Sarah Vowell>Once, he was invited on-stage with Kurt to play along with the song. It was exciting. But he's left-handed. And they didn't have a left-handed guitar for him. So it was sort of a disaster, not the moment a super-fan dreams of.
<v Sarah Vowell>有一次，他被库尔特邀请上台一起演奏。这很令人激动。但他是个左撇子。而他们没有左手吉他给他。所以这算是场灾难，不是那种超级粉丝梦想中的时刻。

00:48:05.510 --> 00:48:19.130
<v Sarah Vowell>So, in this show, what happens is someone tells their little story and then we play a song. So you get to pick which Fastbacks song gets to play at the end of your story.
<v Sarah Vowell>所以，在这个节目里，通常会发生的事是某人讲述他们的小故事，然后我们播放一首歌。所以你可以选择最后在你故事结尾播放哪首Fastbacks的歌。

00:48:19.130 --> 00:48:33.400
<v Scott Lee>At the end of my story? Whew. [LAUGHS] I'd probably say, "Save Room For Me", then. Because I've definitely saved room for the Fastbacks, so, "Save Room For Me".
<v Scott Lee>在我故事结尾？呼。[笑声]那我可能会说《Save Room For Me》（为我留个位置）。因为我确实为Fastbacks留出了位置，所以是《Save Room For Me》。

00:48:33.400 --> 00:48:51.530
<v Scott Lee>[MUSIC PLAYING - "SAVE ROOM FOR ME" BY FASTBACKS]
<v Scott Lee>[音乐播放 - Fastbacks的《Save Room For Me》]

00:48:51.530 --> 00:49:54.330
<v Ira Glass>Sarah Vowell, our contributing editor and the voice of teenage superhero, Violet Parr, in Pixar's new movie, The Incredibles.
<v Ira Glass>萨拉·沃威尔，我们的特约编辑，皮克斯新片《超人总动员》中青少年超级英雄维奥莱特·帕尔的配音演员。

00:49:54.330 --> 00:50:06.070
<v Ira Glass>Act Four, A Life in Music. Sam Franco's 72 and has spent his life playing and teaching the accordion. And the thing to know about him is he hates accordion music, most of it, anyway.
<v Ira Glass>第四幕，音乐人生。萨姆·弗兰科72岁了，一生都在弹奏和教授手风琴。关于他，需要知道的一点是，他讨厌手风琴音乐，或者说大部分都讨厌。

00:50:06.070 --> 00:50:16.100
<v Sam Franco>The sound of the accordion, we don't associate ourselves with too much, is like this.
<v Sam Franco>我们通常联想到的手风琴声音是这样的。

00:50:16.100 --> 00:50:27.550
<v Sam Franco>[ACCORDIAN PLAYING]
<v Sam Franco>[手风琴演奏]

00:50:27.550 --> 00:50:39.230
<v Sam Franco>See? I haven't done that for years. It's hard for me to even play a regular style.
<v Sam Franco>你看？我已经很多年没那样弹过了。我甚至很难按常规风格演奏。

00:50:39.230 --> 00:50:40.400
<v Ira Glass>I see why you like it the other way.
<v Ira Glass>我明白你为什么喜欢另一种弹法了。

00:50:40.400 --> 00:50:49.530
<v Sam Franco>Well, the other way it swings. [LAUGHS] It swings with the one finger. And I get a jazz sound, like this. Watch.
<v Sam Franco>好吧，另一种弹法更有摇摆感。[笑声]那种风格是用一个手指弹奏的。我能弹出一种爵士乐的声音，像这样。看着。

00:50:49.530 --> 00:51:22.990
<v Sam Franco>[ACCORDIAN PLAYING]
<v Sam Franco>[手风琴演奏]

00:51:22.990 --> 00:51:47.150
<v Ira Glass>Sam Franco hates that accordion oom-pah beat. He doesn't like the normal Lawrence Welk chord progressions. In his music, he changes them into jazz chord progressions. And, where normal accordion players use lots of chords all over the place, he prefers to play single notes. In his head, when he plays, Sam hears Coleman Hawkins and other jazz greats. He invented this style. And, if you check the Chicago Yellow Pages for jazz accordion, Sam is the only listing.
<v Ira Glass>萨姆·弗兰科讨厌那种手风琴的“砰-啪”节奏。他不喜欢那种常规的劳伦斯·威尔克式的和弦走向。在他的音乐里，他把它改成了爵士和弦走向。而且，普通手风琴手喜欢到处使用和弦，但他更喜欢弹奏单音。在演奏时，萨姆脑子里听到的全是科尔曼·霍金斯和其他爵士大师的声音。他发明了这种风格。如果你查看芝加哥黄页上的爵士手风琴，萨姆是唯一的条目。

00:51:47.150 --> 00:51:53.440
<v Ira Glass>[ACCORDIAN PLAYING]
<v Ira Glass>[手风琴演奏]

00:51:53.440 --> 00:52:06.620
<v Ira Glass>All this hour we've heard from people who want to make their living, full-time, from their music. They dream and strive for that. Sam's done it all his life, playing gigs, working, never getting famous, though.
<v Ira Glass>这一小时我们听到的都是那些想靠音乐全职谋生的人。他们为此梦想和奋斗。萨姆一生都在做这个，演出、工作，但他从未出名。

00:52:06.620 --> 00:52:20.530
<v Sam Franco>You know what saved me? That I had the knack for teaching. You understand? That was my ace-in-the-hole, would be the teaching part. If I didn't have that, I would be out of the music business completely.
<v Sam Franco>你知道什么救了我吗？我有教书的天赋。你明白吗？那是我的王牌，教书那部分。如果没那个，我早就彻底退出音乐行业了。

00:52:20.530 --> 00:52:32.280
<v Ira Glass>He's watched players who weren't any better than him get a lot of attention, get notoriety, get more gigs. And, in the end, he decided he doesn't care. That life doesn't suit him.
<v Ira Glass>他看着那些水平不如他的人得到了很多关注、声望，得到了更多演出。最终，他决定不在乎了。那种生活不适合他。

00:52:32.280 --> 00:52:47.240
<v Sam Franco>You couldn't pay mortgages, buy a house, on playing because you played one night. That's it. How would you like to go look for a job every week? And that's what it amounted to. I never did have that kind of energy.
<v Sam Franco>你无法靠只在那儿演一场就能付房贷、买房子。就是这样。你愿意每周去到处找工作吗？情况就是如此。我从来没有那种精力。

00:52:47.240 --> 00:52:57.130
<v Sam Franco>[ACCORDIAN PLAYING]
<v Sam Franco>[手风琴演奏]

00:52:57.130 --> 00:53:20.470
<v Ira Glass>Most of the people who Sam started playing with back when he was a kid did not stay with music. Back when Sam began, accordion was huge, huge. Every band in Chicago had an accordionist doing their keyboards because pianos, in those days, were so impractical, you know? You can't carry them around. The one that you find at the hall is always out of tune.
<v Ira Glass>萨姆小时候开始和他一起演奏的大多数人都没有坚持做音乐。萨姆刚开始时，手风琴简直火得不行。芝加哥的每个乐队都有手风琴手来弹键盘部分，因为在那些日子里，钢琴太不方便了，你知道吧？你没法带着它们到处走。礼堂里的那架钢琴总是不准。

00:53:20.470 --> 00:53:34.600
<v Ira Glass>But that era did not last. When Elvis hit in the late '50s and rock and roll followed, accordion business died. Nobody hired them. Nobody wanted to take lessons. And lots of guys who Sam played and taught with quit the music business.
<v Ira Glass>但那个时代并没有持续多久。猫王在50年代末走红，摇滚乐随之而来，手风琴行业就死了。没人雇佣他们，没人想学这个。萨姆一起演奏和教书的那些人，很多都退出了音乐圈。

00:53:34.600 --> 00:54:11.510
<v Ira Glass>Sam had always played guitar too. And he was actually one of the few people in his crowd who made the jump from the swing era to the rock era. These days, he jokes that every brick of his house was paid for by rock and roll. A big poster of electric guitarist, Randy Rhoads, is the biggest thing in his music room. He taught lessons for years on the guitar. But Sam still views the guitar as an inferior instrument. He can lecture you for a long time on how it's not in the symphony orchestra. Basically, in his view, America's 40-year love affair with the guitar is a concerted effort to slap back at the accordion.
<v Ira Glass>萨姆也一直弹吉他。事实上，他是那帮人里少数几个从摇摆时代跨越到摇滚时代的人。这些天，他开玩笑说他房子的每一块砖都是摇滚乐付钱盖的。一张电吉他手兰迪·罗兹的大海报是他音乐室里最大的东西。他教了多年吉他课。但萨姆仍然认为吉他是低劣的乐器。他可以跟你长篇大论它是怎么进不了交响乐团的。在他看来，美国人这40年来对吉他的痴迷，基本上是一场对手风琴的反叛。

00:54:11.510 --> 00:54:58.230
<v Sam Franco>Because everybody's mother and father played the accordion. And they said, we're not going to play it, you know? We're going to play something different. And there it was. It just came out as a rebel cause, I think. It only has five notes, a set of six strings. Two are the same, so it brings it down to five notes, you know? You have to hit it with a pick. You've got to use two hands to make one note. Does that make sense? Why would you want to do that? Why would you want to use two hands to make one note?
<v Sam Franco>因为所有人的爸爸妈妈都拉手风琴。他们说，我们不学那个，你知道吧？我们要弹点不一样的。它就那样出来了，我觉得这是一场反叛。它只有五个音，六根琴弦。两根音一样，所以它只有五个音，你知道吗？你必须用拨片弹。你得用双手去制造一个音。这合理吗？你为什么想那样做？为什么你要用双手去制造一个音呢？

00:54:58.230 --> 00:55:06.800
<v Sam Franco>[ACCORDIAN PLAYING]
<v Sam Franco>[手风琴演奏]

00:55:06.800 --> 00:55:18.020
<v Ira Glass>I met Sam through one of his students, Catherine Boyd, who totally adores Sam, has known him for years and says that, whatever goes on in Sam's life, he's always happy when he's talking about music.
<v Ira Glass>我通过他的学生凯瑟琳·博伊德认识了萨姆，她非常崇拜萨姆，认识他很多年了，她说无论萨姆的生活里发生什么，当他在谈论音乐时，他总是快乐的。

00:55:18.020 --> 00:55:36.140
<v Ira Glass>And the most surprising thing Sam told me when we talked is that it took him a long time before he was completely at peace with the way he plays. He started playing accordion when he was 10. He's 72 now. He played accordions back in the old neighborhood on Taylor Street as a kid. He found his way to the jazz sound that he loves by high school.
<v Ira Glass>萨姆告诉我的最令我惊讶的事是，他花了好长时间才对自己演奏方式彻底安之若素。他10岁开始弹手风琴。他现在72岁。小时候他在泰勒街的老社区里拉手风琴。他在高中时就找到了自己热爱的爵士乐感觉。

00:55:36.140 --> 00:55:58.290
<v Ira Glass>He played in USO shows during World War II. He taught and gigged for the next half century. But he says he never felt good about his playing until he was in his '50s. Up until that point, the sound that he heard in his head wasn't what was coming out of the instrument. In his '50s, he said, he just stopped worrying about what people would say about his playing. He just played.
<v Ira Glass>他在二战期间参加过USO演出。接下来的半个世纪他一直在教学和演出。但他说直到50多岁，他才对自己的演奏感到满意。在那之前，他脑子里听到的声音并没有从乐器里传出来。他说，在50多岁时，他不再担心别人会怎么评论他的演奏。他只是演奏。

00:55:58.290 --> 00:56:13.780
<v Sam Franco>I'd like to say this, is that I never enjoyed playing as much as I do today. In all my days, I never enjoyed playing as much as I do now. So, why is that? Ask me why.
<v Sam Franco>我想说的是，我从未像今天这样享受弹奏。在我所有岁月里，我从未像现在这样享受音乐。所以，为什么呢？问我为什么。

00:56:13.780 --> 00:56:13.950
<v Ira Glass>Why is that?
<v Ira Glass>为什么呢？

00:56:13.950 --> 00:56:45.040
<v Sam Franco>Yeah, why is that? Because, today, I know what I can do. I know my limitations, and I know my abilities. Like, if I can't do it, I say who cares, you know? I can do something else. But, when I was younger, I knew my limitations. Of course, we all know our limitations, and that bothers you. But, today, it doesn't bother me.
<v Sam Franco>是的，为什么呢？因为今天，我知道我能做什么。我知道我的局限性，也知道我的能力。比如，如果我做不到，我就说谁在乎呢，你知道吧？我可以做点别的。但我年轻时，我知道自己的局限。当然，我们都知道自己的局限性，那很困扰你。但今天，它不困扰我了。

00:56:45.040 --> 00:56:51.950
<v Sam Franco>[ACCORDIAN PLAYING]
<v Sam Franco>[手风琴演奏]

00:56:51.950 --> 00:57:01.480
<v Ira Glass>Today, in his music room, surrounded by his instruments, with one of his favorite students, Catherine, egging him on during our interview, Sam couldn't seem happier, really.
<v Ira Glass>今天，在他的音乐室里，被乐器包围着，还有他最喜欢的学生之一凯瑟琳在采访时为他加油，萨姆看起来再开心不过了。

00:57:01.480 --> 00:57:19.850
<v Ira Glass>[ACCORDIAN PLAYING]
<v Ira Glass>[手风琴演奏]

